A||||15||Cheever3000||LOUD screeching sound!!! Alternator?||||4||
Z||000000||Cheever3000||10-23-2001||04:57 PM||||Found some similar posts by searching, but wanted a 2nd opinion.<P>When I got home from work last night my 88GT (stock 2.8) started squealing just as I pulled into the garage.  I didn't think much about it cause it did that once before but was fine the next day.  But I went to leave for work just now and it's sqealing LOUD, even with the A/C turned off.<P>I'm thinking alternator because it quit on me for a few days recently (about 2 months ago), but suddenly started working again.  This car likes to scare me like that.  So I found someone with a used alternator for cheap but last I heard he was looking into the shipping cost.  <P>I was looking down in there, and then took the right rear wheel off, but geez I don't see how you guys replace those things!  One guy in a recent thread said the noise went away when he sprayed WD40 on the alt shaft, but his wasn't as loud as mine.<P>Sure do wish the hydraulic line on the Coupe wasn't busted!  This may be the first time both of my Fieros have been broke.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>||||reg||4
Z||000001||Cheever3000||10-23-2001||05:15 PM||||Can I use the alternator off my 4-cylinder Coupe?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||10-23-2001||05:19 PM||||Could it be the alt belt?  I'm having a hell of a time with mine.  It takes about 3 days for the belt to stretch enough to start squealing again.  Back to the shop for them to tighten it up.  Repeat this for a couple of weeks, then replace the belt.  Anyone have any ideas?  Possibly a Kevlar belt?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fierozilla||10-23-2001||05:21 PM||||Its your alt belt. Very common prob. Watch your volts guage (if applicable) when the squeeking begins, and you should see it drop.<BR>Use a DAYCO brand alt belt, it'll stop the squeeking.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mach10||10-23-2001||06:06 PM||||If the pulleys are out of alignment, that belt will be continuously squealing, stopping when you re-tension, and squealing again within the week. Use a large enough set-squar in the middle of the grooves of the pulley, and see if they line up. Flocked if I know what to do if they aren't tho  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000005||Spektrum-87GT||10-23-2001||06:11 PM||||while the topic is up... how the heck do you tighten it!? i have three pulleys... the crankshaft, the alternator, and the water pump... but there's no tensioner! i got the belt, but puzzled where to begin. the haynes doesnt cover it.||||reg||1
Z||000006||WampusCat||10-23-2001||06:18 PM||||First loosen the pivot bolt on the alt.(top bolt)  Then loosen the bottom bolt on the adjusting bracket, reached through the wheel well with the wheel removed.  Then take a LARGE screwdriver or prybar and hold pressure on the alt. to tighten the belt.  Then tighten the bottom bolt.  It helps a lot if you have someone to hold tension of the alt. while you tighten the bolt.  Then tighten the pivot bolt and you're done.||||reg||1
Z||000007||87GTZ34||10-23-2001||09:54 PM||||It could be just the belt or you may have developed an oil leak from the front of the crank or cover and it is soaking on the pulley or belt.  Or... you may have a coolant leak and it is doing the same thing.  MM, I went through 3 cycles but mine stopped squealing.  I have fixed all oil & coolant leaks though.  GL||||reg||1
Z||000008||Fiero*101||10-23-2001||10:37 PM||||Yeah Cheever 3000<BR>there a pain to change. After you take out the tire well and the rubber you need to take off the ty rod too and pull it back so that when you do get the alt bracket off then the Alt should come out w/ some turining around and up side down positions the alt should come out  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Fiero*101<BR>Gold 86 SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000009||camon||10-24-2001||03:31 AM||||cheever 3000:<P>If you were messing around the therostat anytime rescently (like a day or so before the squeal started) try WD-40 and spray the alt shaft (as coolant may have run down over your alternator) . Mine was squealing pretty bad (when I started it and as the car got warm it got louder). This may not be your problem....but it is worth a shot if you haven't tried it yet, WD-40 is really cheap stuff.<P>Good luck!<P>------------------<BR>Keith||||reg||1
Z||000010||SLYGUY||10-24-2001||03:38 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Spektrum-87GT:<BR><B>while the topic is up... how the heck do you tighten it!? i have three pulleys... the crankshaft, the alternator, and the water pump... but there's no tensioner! i got the belt, but puzzled where to begin. the haynes doesnt cover it.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>After you remove the passenger side rear wheel and put the car up on a jack AND jackstands you can look up behind the inner wheel well plastic and see the nut and bolt that you will use to adjust tension. <BR>Just loosen up the nut, have a buddy use a crowbar on the alternator from up above, and retighten. <BR>Took me a couple of attempts to get the tension right. Not difficult once you get past all the ancillary prep work i.e. jackstands and wheel removal.<P>Tackle that sucker. Just dive right in....I did. Not difficult when you look at it that way.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000011||Cheever3000||10-24-2001||02:01 PM||||Just remove the wheel well, huh?  Uh-huh.  Took me an hour to do that alone.  Hope I can get it back on later, I hate those stupid plastic anchors.<P>Got the lower bolt out.  Got the upper bolt loose but there's a shield in the way of removing it entirely, and the alt won't come down without it being out.  The nut holding on the shield is a strange size.  13mm is too big and 12 is too small.  The metal is too thick to use tin snips on it, and too stiff to bend it.  Might have to use the BFH.<P>That's all my back will let me do today.<BR>||||reg||8
Z||000012||Cheever3000||10-27-2001||12:27 PM||||YAY!!! It was just the belt... WOO-HOO!<BR>My son is out there now putting the wheelwell back in.  Guess I'll have to give him a driving lesson for his help (pray!).<P>BTW, there are <B>no</B> Dayco belts in Houston, period.  Went with a Gates w/ lifetime warranty, from O'Reilly's.<P>Well, my son's finished already, gonna get it off the jackstands and DRIVE that sucker!<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000013||Cheever3000||10-27-2001||01:05 PM||||Well, the noise came back by the time I got to the end of the block.  Maybe I didn't get it tight enough.  <P>Oh, I just realized I forgot to tighten the top bolt!!! Sheesh, what an idiot.  Back out to the garage...<BR>||||reg||13
Z||000014||Cheever3000||10-27-2001||05:51 PM||||Now I got it TOO tight.  Squeals as bad as ever and started smoking and smelled like burning rubber... I almost caught that belt on fire!<P>I hate working on cars.  Have I mentioned that before?<P>Got another alternator on the way.  I'll try replacing it, but if it doesn't help this car is getting towed to the shop.  I'm not a happy camper right now.  I'm too tired and sore to throw anything.<P>||||reg||8
Z||000015||theogre||10-28-2001||01:37 PM||||If the belt cooked or is otheriwse rapidly crapping, then there's a fried bearing on something. The belt(s) will keep right on cooking untill this problem is fixed.<P>One aide to tightening an alternator is actually the alternator itself... If you look at the ear for the "top" bolt, you'll see it has flat sides.<P>These flats can be used with an open end wrench or "crow foot" socket to tighten the alternator. Crow foot sockets look like an open end wrench mated to a square drive hole. They can be used with ratchets and extension bars to reach allot of stuff you can't do with a regular socket or straight wrench.<P>I don't know what size crow foot the GM alternators use. I can't get to mine to measure it right now. If someone has an alternator out, please see what wrench(es) fits that ear snug and post the result.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
